<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Himilce</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">3rd century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · Also known as Imilce</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Himilce, also called Imilce, was an Iberian woman from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castulo">Castulo</a>, in the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadalquivir">Guadalquivir</a> valley, who married the Carthaginian general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> records that Hannibal's wife came from Castulo, and the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silius-italicus">Silius Italicus</a> gives her name. The marriage reflects the way the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcid-family">Barcid family</a> bound the leading Iberian communities to Carthaginian rule.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/himilce.jpg" alt="Himilce" width="320" height="479" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jl FilpoC · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Himilce,_princesa_de_C%C3%A1stulo_(Baeza).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
